WEBVTT

00:00:00.027 --> 00:00:05.752
Three ideas appear repeatedly. A scalar subquery behaves like one value.

00:00:05.752 --> 00:00:11.542
A set subquery returns zero or more values, usually from one column.

00:00:11.542 --> 00:00:19.102
A correlated subquery refers to the current outer row, so its result can change as the outer row

00:00:19.102 --> 00:00:25.932
changes. When you identify the shape and dependency, the SQL operator becomes easier to choose.

00:00:25.932 --> 00:00:28.352
A subquery has a contract: it returns a shape.

00:00:28.352 --> 00:00:35.057
A scalar subquery must return at most one value where it is used.

00:00:35.057 --> 00:00:42.367
Before writing syntax, predict whether the inner query returns one value, one column with many rows,

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:48.760
or a table. Shape mistakes are easier to prevent than to debug after the query becomes large.
